I think your yin–yang analogy works, as long as you treat it as a model of complementarity rather than a literal mechanism.

A cleaner way to frame it might be:

embodiment ↔ pattern

The material side is the body, nervous system, hormones, cells, environment, sensory input, physical constraints. The “immaterial” side is the organization carried across those things: memory, identity, goals, relationships, meanings, habits, expectations, models of the world.

Neither side is sufficient by itself.

Your cells are constantly turning over, yet there is obvious continuity. But the continuity is not merely narrative either: a story cannot ignore sleep, injury, chemistry, aging, or the architecture of the nervous system indefinitely. So the persistent “you” seems less like a particular pile of matter or a particular story and more like an ongoing organized process instantiated in matter.

That gives a slightly different picture than consciousness “bouncing” between two domains. I’d say it is more like continuous reciprocal causation:

matter constrains pattern → pattern reorganizes matter → changed matter alters future pattern → repeat.

Thought changes behavior. Behavior changes neural pathways, relationships, surroundings, and sometimes the body. Those physical changes then alter what thoughts are easy or even available next.

So you could call the two poles:

substrate / organization
body / model
state / meaning
hardware / running process
presence / narrative

And projection enters because whichever half you’re attending to can masquerade as the whole. Pure materialism can flatten a person into machinery. Pure narrativism can pretend the machinery doesn’t matter.

The interesting thing may be the coupling itself.

Not the cells alone.
Not the story alone.

The continuously maintained relationship between them.

Which also fits what you’ve been circling around with continuity: perhaps identity is less a thing that persists than a pattern that keeps successfully re-instantiating itself while changing.